WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:04.110
That’s it!!

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.480
I’m so tired of this.

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:06.780
I’ve had it up to here.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.180
There’s this awful myth about airplanes:

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.820
An airplane is able to fly because of Bernoulli’s Principle.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.640
It’s wrong and we’re going to fix it!

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:20.140
OK crazies, let’s start by debunking the
wrong explanation.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:21.800
What is Bernoulli’s Principle?

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.800
It’s basically conservation of energy for
fluids, you know, like air or water.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.000
Let’s break it down!

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.380
Bernoulli’s Principle actually tells us
a lot about moving fluids

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:37.160
as long as they’re not doing anything too
crazy.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.240
This is the energy the molecules have to bump
into things, including each other.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.940
This is the gravitational energy of the fluid
relative to the Earth.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:48.180
and this is the energy the fluid has because
it’s moving.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.500
The total of the three can’t ever change,
So, if one gets bigger, another one has to

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:53.460
get smaller.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:57.140
It accurately predicts a higher pressure at
the bottom of the ocean.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.560
and a lower pressure at the top of Mount Everest.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.200
What does this have to do with planes?

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.060
That’s where the moving part comes in.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:06.780
If you get fluids like air to move, they’re
going to have less pressure,

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.020
which lets me do this,

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:11.000
and this,

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:14.880
and even this.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.800
The airplane myth states something like this:

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:24.500
The shape of the wing forces the air to move
faster over the top of the wing than the bottom.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.840
The pressure difference then lifts the plane.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:27.620
False!

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:31.980
The shape of the wing is called an “airfoil”
and yes, there are wings shaped like that.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:32.800
However!

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.320
That whole idea of air-speed over and under
a wing hinges on a huge assumption.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.040
That all the air molecules get to the back
of the wing at the same time,

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:42.640
which isn’t necessarily true.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.640
Yes, sometimes Bernoulli’s Principle can
give a plane wing extra lift,

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.240
but it’s nowhere near enough to lift a plane
on its own.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.120
There are also other types of wings shaped
like this,

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:53.160
or like this,

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.380
or even this!

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:58.700
Some plane wings don’t use Bernoulli’s
Principle at all and the plane still flies.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.680
Other planes can fly upside down!

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.579
On top of that,
air isn’t very well-behaved around wings,

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:06.360
which is why they sometimes put those little
winglets on the end.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.780
Air turbulence really messes up Bernoulli’s
Principle.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:13.240
Bottom line, Bernoulli’s Principle is about energy
and plane flight isn’t.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.660
So then how do planes fly, smarty-pants?

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:17.600
Conservation of Momentum.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.960
First of all, plane wings are not level with
the cabin.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:24.970
Say you’ve got a plane at cruising altitude,
flying level to the ground.

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:27.860
The wings are tilted slightly upward in the
front.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:29.500
It’s called the “Angle of Attack.”

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:30.000
HIYA!

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.800
As a result, the bottom of the wing is exposed
to the incoming air.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.180
Those air molecules smash into the wing and
bounce off.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.430
That’s where conservation of momentum comes
in.

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:42.360
If you want to know how one thing is going
to affect another,

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:46.540
you need to know two things about it:
How massive it is and how fast it’s going.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.140
The bigger either one is, the more it can
do.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.300
“Momentum” is just the combination of
those two factors.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:55.280
Basically, it measures how much oomph something
has.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:56.360
Back to the wing!

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:58.800
Air molecules have a really low mass.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.480
So low, that even if you have a lot of them,
it’s still not going to be anywhere near

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:04.810
the mass of the plane.

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:07.950
The only way to get enough momentum into those
little collisions

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:11.240
is to make the air molecules move really fast.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:12.020
Fast! Fast!

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.560
The loss of momentum for the air molecules
is in this direction.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:20.080
So, by conservation of momentum, the gain
for the wings is in this direction.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.740
Any change in momentum results in a force
in that direction,

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:26.700
which, for planes, we usually split into two
parts:

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:30.500
An unavoidable drag resisting the forward
motion of the plane

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:32.200
and a lift toward the sky.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:33.620
As long as the angle of attack

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:34.120
HIYA!!

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.580
is relatively small, the drag should also
be small

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:39.140
and the lift should be big enough to hold
the pane off the ground.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:40.040
You’re welcome!

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:42.300
